\bottomrule
\multicolumn{3}{@{}l@{}}{\textcolor{blue}{\textit{Values: mean ± SD (n=10). ***p<0.05, ns=not significant}}}\\
\multicolumn{3}{@{}l@{}}{\textit{Negative ATP consumption indicates net ATP accumulation}}\\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table \ref{tab:thermodynamic_comparison} summarizes key metrics \textcolor{blue}{across ten replicate simulations per condition}. \textcolor{blue}{\textbf{Key findings:} (1) Sporulation yield is \textbf{nearly identical} between conditions (59.1 ± 8.3 mM vs 57.6 ± 7.8 mM, p>0.05), demonstrating \textbf{robust completion} despite 17-fold ATP difference. (2) ATP dynamics differ significantly (p<0.05): normal conditions show net consumption (341 ± 511 mM), while stress shows net \textbf{accumulation} (-238 ± 284 mM), indicating bypass pathway activation of ATP regeneration. (3) Both conditions achieve 100\% success rate (20/20 runs) with perfect layer activation (6/6), validating thermodynamic hierarchy robustness. The high ATP variability (CV=150\%) reflects stochastic KinA entry kinetics (320 ± 182 firings), while spore output remains consistent (CV=14\%), demonstrating that thermodynamic constraints buffer stochastic regulatory noise.}

\subsection{Constraint-Based Pathway Selection}

To understand mechanistically why hierarchical preemption occurs, we analyzed ATP-dependence across transitions (Table \ref{tab:atp_dependence}):

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{ATP Dependence of Layers}
\label{tab:atp_dependence}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{L} & \textbf{Transition} & \textbf{ATP} & \textbf{Stress} \\
\midrule
0 & Spo0F-P & Hi ($n$=2) & 99.6\% ↓ \\
0 & Spo0A~P & Hi ($n$=1) & 94\% ↓ \\
1 & $\sigma$H txn & Med & Suppr. \\
2 & Septation & Hi & t=5.3s \\
3 & $\sigma$F act. & None & t=0.03s \\
4 & $\sigma$E act. & None & t=0.44s \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Statistical mechanics interpretation:} At ATP = 300 mM, the probability of ATP-dependent transitions firing is:
\begin{equation}
    P(t_{\text{ATP-dep}}) \propto \exp\left(-\frac{\Delta G^\ddagger}{k_B T}\right) \cdot [\text{ATP}]^n \approx 0.06^n
\end{equation}

For $n=2$: $P \approx 0.0036$ (99.6\% suppression)

ATP-independent transitions remain unaffected:
\begin{equation}
    P(t_{\text{ATP-indep}}) \propto \exp\left(-\frac{\Delta G_0^\ddagger}{k_B T}\right) \approx 1
\end{equation}

Thus, the system follows the \textit{most probable path} given thermodynamic constraints—a direct consequence of statistical mechanics applied to reaction networks.

\subsection{Irreversibility and Entropy Production}

Post-commitment (t > 0.44 s), the stress trajectory exhibits no reversal despite ATP regeneration (300 → 893 mM by t=60s). This thermodynamic irreversibility arises from:


\textbf{Entropy production:} 766 transition firings generate $\Delta S > 0$
\textbf{Forward bias:} SigmaE activation creates autocatalytic commitment loop
\textbf{Barrier asymmetry:} Reverse barrier $\Delta G^\ddagger_{\text{rev}} \gg \Delta G^\ddagger_{\text{fwd}}$


From the Second Law:
\begin{equation}
    \Delta S_{\text{total}} = \Delta S_{\text{system}} + \Delta S_{\text{env}} > 0
\end{equation}

Where:

$\Delta S_{\text{system}} < 0$ (increased order: spore formation)
$\Delta S_{\text{env}} > 0$ (ATP hydrolysis, heat dissipation)


Net entropy increase locks the decision, preventing spontaneous reversal. ATP regeneration (+593 mM net increase from 300 to 893 mM over 60 s) occurs post-commitment and cannot reverse the sporulation trajectory.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Thermodynamics as Mechanism for Hierarchical Preemption}

Our results establish that \textbf{thermodynamic constraints actively drive hierarchical preemption} through three mechanisms:


\textbf{ATP-dependent rate modulation:} $r \propto [\text{ATP}]^n$ creates 94--99.6\% suppression of phosphorylation cascades at low ATP
\textbf{Pathway accessibility filtering:} Only ATP-independent routes remain thermodynamically accessible
\textbf{Statistical mechanical selection:} System follows most probable path given constraints


This is fundamentally different from regulatory bypass (e.g., crosstalk, feedback inhibition) because suppression occurs at the \textit{thermodynamic level}—reactions are kinetically blocked regardless of regulatory state.

\subsection{The Role of Hybrid Hierarchical Petri Nets}

This discovery was enabled by the hybrid hierarchical Petri net formalism, which uniquely integrates:


\textbf{Discrete regulatory logic:} Places/transitions capture molecular species and reactions
\textbf{Continuous energy dynamics:} Real-valued markings enable ATP/GTP tracking
\textbf{Resource-aware concurrency:} Arc weights and inhibitor arcs encode stoichiometric/thermodynamic constraints \citep{qian2005,slepchenko2003,koch2011}
\textbf{Hierarchical structure:} Signal flow arcs represent layer dependencies


Classical approaches fail to capture this:

\textbf{ODEs:} No explicit resource competition or concurrent pathway evaluation
\textbf{Boolean networks:} Binary states cannot represent continuous energy depletion
\textbf{Stochastic simulation:} Computationally prohibitive for rare event (stress) analysis
\textbf{Constraint-based models (FBA):} Assume steady-state, miss transient hierarchical dynamics


\textbf{The hybrid hierarchical Petri net formalism provides the necessary and sufficient mathematical framework for this analysis}: tokens are conserved quantities (ATP, species), transitions are energy-coupled reactions, and arc weights are stoichiometric coefficients. This is not merely a modeling convenience—\textit{it is the correct formalism for systems where resource competition determines behavior}.

\subsection{Unified Framework: Signal Hierarchy + Thermodynamics}

\textcolor{blue}{We propose a unified framework where \textbf{ATP functions as a hierarchical signal}, not merely a metabolic substrate. Signal Hierarchical Control orchestrates cellular decision-making, with thermodynamics as the enforcement mechanism:}

\textbf{ATP as Dual-Function Signal}

\textcolor{blue}{ATP operates simultaneously as (1) a thermodynamic resource (enabling energy-coupled reactions) and (2) a hierarchical signal (gating layer activation). The free energy of ATP hydrolysis, $\Delta G_{\text{ATP}} = \Delta G^{\circ'} + RT \ln([\text{ADP}][\text{P}_i]/[\text{ATP}])$, couples ATP concentration to reaction feasibility. When ATP saturates (high [ATP] → $\Delta G_{\text{ATP}} \approx -54$ kJ/mol), all layers activate normally—the canonical cascade emerges. When ATP depletes (low [ATP] → $\Delta G_{\text{ATP}} \approx -21$ kJ/mol), thermodynamic constraints collapse the hierarchy by making ATP-dependent reactions kinetically inaccessible, forcing ATP-independent pathway selection. Thus, Signal Hierarchical Control is the orchestrator, with thermodynamics measuring ($\Delta G$, entropy production) and enforcing (reaction barriers) ATP's signaling state. The 17.8-fold reduction in entropy production rate (1432 → 80.4 J/(mol·K·s)) quantitatively reflects hierarchy collapse.}

\textbf{ATP Saturation as Thermodynamic Equilibrium}

\textcolor{blue}{ATP saturation is thermodynamically analogous to thermal equilibrium. Just as temperature diffusion ceases when thermal equilibrium is reached ($\nabla T = 0$), ATP saturation represents uniform chemical potential across all cellular compartments and reactions. In this state, the ATP chemical potential $\mu_{\text{ATP}} = \mu^{\circ}_{\text{ATP}} + RT \ln[\text{ATP}]$ is uniformly high, providing the same thermodynamic driving force ($\Delta G \approx -54$ kJ/mol) to all ATP-dependent reactions—the hierarchy functions normally with all layers accessible. Depletion creates a chemical potential gradient: reactions with high activation barriers become thermodynamically forbidden while low-barrier reactions remain accessible. This is analogous to heat flow following temperature gradients: signal propagation follows ATP chemical potential gradients. The stress pathway emerges because ATP-independent reactions remain accessible regardless of $\mu_{\text{ATP}}$, creating a thermodynamically favored route when the ATP gradient collapses the canonical hierarchy.}

\textbf{Hierarchies are Thermodynamic Constructs}

Biological hierarchies emerge from differential energy requirements across signaling layers, not from hard-coded regulatory sequences.

\textbf{Energy as Hierarchical Selector}

Energy availability acts as a \textit{selector} determining which layers can activate, with low energy enabling preemption of ATP-independent routes.

\textbf{Constraint-Based Emergence}

Decision pathways follow statistical mechanics: the system selects the most probable route given thermodynamic constraints, maximizing entropy production.

\textbf{Adaptive ATP Economy}

\textcolor{blue}{Stress pathways evolve to minimize pre-commitment ATP consumption (16× reduction), optimizing survival under resource scarcity through regulatory overhead elimination rather than improved energy coupling efficiency. This reflects the fundamental principle that bacterial growth and survival depend on balancing anabolic and catabolic energy costs \citep{russell1995,setlow2006}.}

This framework explains diverse phenomena:

\textbf{Quorum sensing:} Low ATP blocks autoinducer synthesis, preventing premature commitment
\textbf{Phage lysis/lysogeny:} Energy status gates Cro vs CI dominance
\textbf{Apoptosis:} Low ATP triggers necrosis (unregulated) vs high ATP enabling apoptosis (regulated)
\textbf{Cell cycle checkpoints:} G1/S and G2/M gates are ATP thresholds


\subsection{Evolutionary Implications}

\textcolor{blue}{The 16-fold ATP economy improvement (reduced consumption) of the stress pathway suggests strong selection pressure for dual-mode decision systems:}


\textbf{Normal mode (high ATP):} Energy-expensive phosphorylation cascades provide multiple checkpoints, enabling reversibility and graded responses
\textcolor{blue}{\textbf{Stress mode (low ATP):} ATP-economical bypass enables rapid irreversible commitment when resources are scarce, skipping checkpoint overhead}


\textcolor{blue}{This \textbf{adaptive thermodynamic architecture} maximizes fitness across environmental conditions: gradual decision-making under abundance, rapid commitment under scarcity. The 16× difference reflects regulatory cost (Layers 0--2 phosphorylation overhead) rather than improved thermodynamic work conversion.}

From an evolutionary perspective, the ATP-independent pathway likely evolved first (Layer 3--5 as primordial stress response), with the phosphorylation cascade (Layer 0--2) layered on top to provide regulatory control under favorable conditions—explaining why stress "inverts" to the ancestral state.

\subsection{Predictive Framework}

Our thermodynamic framework generates testable predictions:

\textbf{Critical ATP minimum}

The 1 mM ATP critical minimum observed in stress conditions should represent a universal lower limit for energy-dependent sporulation across \textit{Bacillus} species, below which commitment cannot proceed despite regeneration.

\textbf{Knockout phenotypes}

Deleting ATP-dependent transitions (KinA, Spo0F) should not prevent stress-induced sporulation but will block normal pathway commitment.

\textbf{Temperature dependence}

Commitment rate should follow Arrhenius kinetics: $k \propto \exp(-\Delta G^\ddagger / RT)$, enabling barrier measurement from temperature-dependent commitment times.

\textbf{Intermediate ATP levels}

ATP 500--2000 mM should show \textit{mixed mode} where both pathways partially activate, creating bistability.

\subsection{Limitations and Future Directions}

\textbf{Current limitations:}

Model parameters are estimated from literature; direct experimental validation needed
Free energy landscape reconstruction assumes ergodicity (sufficient sampling)
Spatial heterogeneity (cell-to-cell variability) not fully captured


\subsection{Experimental Validation Requirements}

\textcolor{blue}{This work presents computational predictions that require experimental validation. Key testable predictions include:}

\textcolor{blue}{\textbf{ATP dynamics:} Single-cell ATP imaging (e.g., FRET-based ATP sensors \citep{berg2009}) during sporulation onset should reveal:}
\textcolor{blue}{\begin{itemize}
    \item Normal pathway: ATP remains 4000--5000 mM throughout commitment
    \item Stress pathway: ATP drops to $\sim$1--10 mM during commitment, then recovers
    \item Critical threshold: Commitment fails when ATP $< 1$ mM despite regeneration
\end{itemize}}

\textcolor{blue}{\textbf{ATP economy:} Quantitative measurements of total ATP consumption per spore (via $^{32}$P-ATP incorporation or bioluminescence) should show:}
\textcolor{blue}{\begin{itemize}
    \item Normal conditions: 10--15 mM ATP/spore (model: 11.6 mM)
    \item Stress conditions: 0.5--1.5 mM ATP/spore (model: 0.73 mM)
    \item Economy ratio: 10--20× reduction under stress (model: 16×)
\end{itemize}}

\textcolor{blue}{\textbf{Hierarchical preemption:} Time-resolved fluorescence microscopy of sigma factor activation (using fluorescent reporters for SigmaF, SigmaE, SigmaH) should reveal:}
\textcolor{blue}{\begin{itemize}
    \item Normal pathway: Sequential activation (Layer 0 → 1 → 2 → 3 → 4)
    \item Stress pathway: Inverted sequence (Layers 3--5 activate before 0--2)
    \item Layer bypass: SigmaF activation independent of SigmaH under stress
\end{itemize}}

\textcolor{blue}{\textbf{Pathway commitment barriers:} Temperature-dependent commitment kinetics should follow:}
\textcolor{blue}{\begin{equation}
    k_{\text{commitment}} = A \exp\left(-\frac{\Delta G^\ddagger}{RT}\right)
\end{equation}}
\textcolor{blue}{enabling direct measurement of activation barriers predicted by our free energy landscapes (5--15 $k_B T$ depending on ATP levels).}

\textcolor{blue}{Until these validations are performed, our results should be interpreted as mechanistic hypotheses and quantitative predictions rather than experimentally confirmed phenomena.}


\textbf{Implemented capabilities:}

\textbf{Hybrid stochastic simulation:} $\tau$-leaping algorithm with Skellam distribution for reversible reactions enables efficient simulation of low-copy regulatory species alongside continuous metabolic fluxes
\textbf{Flux balance analysis:} Integrated FBA module validates steady-state feasibility and identifies blocked reactions through linear programming constraints


\textbf{Future extensions:}

\textbf{Spatial extension:} Token diffusion for spatial pattern formation in heterogeneous cell populations
\textbf{Genome-scale integration:} Couple with genome-scale metabolic models for comprehensive energy accounting across all pathways
\textbf{Experimental validation:} Single-cell ATP imaging during sporulation onset to directly measure commitment barriers and validate thermodynamic landscape predictions


\section{Conclusions}

\textcolor{blue}{Our computational model demonstrates that \textbf{thermodynamic constraints drive hierarchical preemption} in cellular decision-making, where energy availability determines pathway accessibility through ATP-dependent reaction kinetics. Using hybrid Petri nets—combining stochastic transitions for regulatory events with continuous sources for metabolic flux—simulations predict that \textit{Bacillus subtilis} stress-induced sporulation inverts the canonical five-layer cascade, achieving 16-fold better ATP economy (0.73 vs 11.6 mM ATP/spore) via rapid ATP-independent commitment that bypasses regulatory checkpoint overhead.}

This work establishes three key principles:

\textbf{Biological hierarchies are emergent thermodynamic constructs}, not hard-coded regulatory sequences
\textbf{Energy acts as hierarchical selector}, gating layer activation through constraint-based pathway filtering
\textbf{Decision-making follows statistical mechanics}, selecting most probable routes given thermodynamic constraints


\textcolor{blue}{The hybrid hierarchical Petri net formalism provides a natural mathematical framework for this analysis, integrating stochastic regulatory events with continuous metabolic dynamics in a resource-aware concurrency model that captures both discrete signaling decisions and continuous energy flow.} \textcolor{blue}{Rigorous thermodynamic analysis confirms that hierarchical preemption reduces ATP consumption by 16×, reflecting elimination of phosphorylation cascade overhead (Layers 0--2) rather than improved energy coupling efficiency per se.} This represents a paradigm shift from viewing metabolism as background to recognizing thermodynamic constraints as \textit{active organizing principles} in biological information processing. Application to \textit{Bacillus subtilis} sporulation demonstrates that biological hierarchies are emergent thermodynamic constructs where energy status acts as hierarchical selector, enabling rapid stress-adapted decision-making through constraint-based pathway selection.

